          %h1#contents Contents
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#HistoryAndPerspective"} History and perspective
              %li
                %a{:href => "#Statement"} Statement
              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            What has been called quantum “teleportation” (
            %a{:href => "#BBCJPW93"}> BBCJPW 1993
            ) is a procedure (a “protocol”) in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            by which the exact transmission of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
            (such as that of some
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qbits"} qbits
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical
            information,
            %em provided
            that sender and receiver “share a
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bell+state"} Bell state
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subsystems"} subsystems
            which have been
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state+preparation"} prepared
            in a maximally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entanglement"} entangled
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
            with each other. The protocol involves a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            and its ensuing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunction+collapse"} wavefunction collapse
            destroys (“uses up”) this entangled state in order to transmit the remaining quantum state – which somewhat relativizes the imagery of “teleportation”.
          %p
            While this prose may appear somewhat exotic, the actual mathematics underlying the “quantum teleportation protocol” is an elementary and direct consequence of the most basic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axioms"} axioms
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            (in fact it is all but a self-evident consequence when formulated in modern notation, see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#HistoryAndPerspective"} below
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/information+theory"} information theoretic
            perspective on it.
          %h2#HistoryAndPerspective History and perspective
          %p
            Given that the basic axioms of quantum physics were established in the 1920s and widely appreciated by the 1930s, the observation of the possibility of “quantum teleportation” only
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BBCJPW93"} in 1993
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Coecke10"} Coecke 2010, p. 1
          %blockquote
            %p why did it take us 60 years to discover the conceptually intriguing and easily derivable physical phenomenon of ‘quantum teleportation’?
          %p
            Traditional reviews of the matter maintain that the phenomenon just happens to be intrinsically tricky to see through – e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Aaronson18"} Aaronson 2018
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://www.scottaaronson.com/qclec.pdf#page=71"} p. 71
          %blockquote
            %p These sorts of protocols can be hard to find.
          %p
            On the other hand, it was observed around
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Coecke04"} Coecke 2004
            %a{:href => "#AbramskyCoecke04"} Abramsky &amp; Coecke 2004
            – and pronouncedly brought out in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Coecke05"} Coecke 2005
            %a{:href => "#Coecke10"} 2010
            – that the logical structure of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            in general and that of quantum teleportation in particular is transparently captured by
            = succeed "-calculus" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+category"} tensor category
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+space"} finite dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            (see
            = succeed "):" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+quantum+mechanics+in+terms+of+dagger-compact+categories"} finite quantum mechanics in terms of dagger-compact categories
          %p
            In this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category-theoretic
            language, the quantum teleportation protocol is essentially just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zig-zag+identity"} zig-zag identity
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dualizable+objects"} dualizable objects
            (here:
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+space"} finite-dimensional
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+spaces"} Hilbert spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#StatementInStringDiagramLanguage"} below
          %p
            This suggests the following alternative answer, from
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#Coecke05"} Coecke 2005, p. 1
          %blockquote
            %p Why did discovering quantum teleportation take 60 year?
            %p
              We claim that this is due to a ‘bad quantum formalism’ (bad
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≠
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \neq
              wrong) and this badness is in particular due to the fact that the formalism is ‘too low level’.
          %p
            Here “bad quantum formalism” refers to the traditional notation as in the original
            %a{:href => "#BBCJPW93"} BBCJPW93
            and in traditional reviews (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Aaronson18"} Aaronson 2018
            %a{:href => "#Coecke05"} Coecke 05
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/pdf/quant-ph/0510032.pdf#page=8"} ftn. 5 on p. 8
            = succeed "-calculus" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+category"} tensor category
            of finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces, further discussed at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+quantum+mechanics+in+terms+of+dagger-compact+categories"} finite quantum mechanics in terms of dagger-compact categories
          %h2#Statement Statement
          %p#StatementInStringDiagramLanguage
            The quantum teleportation protocol is neatly expressed in
            = succeed "-calculus" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "#Coecke05"} Coecke 2005
          %p
            %img{:src => "/nlab/files/QuantumTeleportationProtocol-220906b.jpg", :width => "850"}/
          %p
            Here, by the general laws of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+space"} finite dimensional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi.mathscript ℋ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ≃
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H} \,\simeq\, \mathbb{C}^D
            (of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension+of+a+vector+space"} dimension
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+vector+space"} dual vector space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+Hilbert+spaces"} tensor product
            of the corresponding Hilbert spaces, and the curve “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊃
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \supset
            ” denotes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/evaluation"} evaluation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi.mathscript ℋ
                      %mo *
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi ev
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H}^\ast \otimes \mathscr{H} \xrightarrow{ev} \mathbb{C}
          %p
            The wiggly line denotes a classically
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/controlled+quantum+gate"} controlled quantum gate
            = succeed "-outcome" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
          %p
            That – for any fixed measurement outcome at the top with the correspondingly controlled correction gate operation at the bottom – this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            (represents a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+map"} linear map
            which) takes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+state"} quantum state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi
            from “Agent A” (“Alice”) to the same state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Psi
            for “Agent B” (“Bob”), as shown is
            %em immediate
            from the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zig-zag+identity"} zig-zag identity
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity"} identity
            line, without changing the operational value of the diagram. This is the transparent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof"} proof
            of the quantum teleporation protocol.
          %p
            Notice that this is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigorous+proof"} rigorous proof
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagram"} string diagram
            is close to informal flow-charts traditionally used to illustrate the idea of quantum teleportation (e.g
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BouwmeesterEkertZeilinger20"} Bouwmeester, Ekert &amp; Zeilinger 2020, Fig. 3.1
          %p
            In more detail, here the top box shows the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projector"} projector
            onto a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bell+state"} Bell state
            with one variable transformed by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operator"} unitary operator
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+measurement"} quantum measurement
            in a corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+basis"} linear basis
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi.mathscript ℋ
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi.mathscript ℋ
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H} \otimes \mathscr{H}^\ast
            (according to the
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wavefunction+collapse"} collapse postulate
          %p
            Concretely, if the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            represented by a single solid line is that of a single
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/qbit"} qbit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi.mathscript ℋ
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathscr{H} = \mathbb{C}^2
            (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = 2
            ), then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            may be taken to range over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+operator"} unitary
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pauli+matrices"} Pauli matrices
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi σ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ≔
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "center center", :displaystyle => "false", :rowspacing => "0.5ex"}
                      %mtr
